\begin{frontmatter}

\title{VMAF-Aware Runtime Shielding for Constrained Deep RL\\in Adaptive Video Streaming}

\author[uta]{Saeed Zarbi}
\author[uta]{Leili Farzinvash}
\author[uta]{Pedram Salehpour\corref{c1}}
\cortext[c1]{Corresponding author}
\ead[c1]{psalehpour@tabrizu.ac.ir}

\address[uta]{Department of Computer Engineering, Faculty of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Tabriz, Tabriz, Iran}

\begin{abstract}
Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) policies for client-side Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming can achieve high average Quality of Experience (QoE) yet concentrate catastrophic rebuffer risk in the tail, particularly under 5G/mmWave throughput volatility.
Conventional runtime shields mitigate stalls by uniformly decrementing the bitrate index, but this content-blind strategy sacrifices perceptual fidelity in proportion to the non-linear VMAF spacing of the encoding ladder.

We formulate ABR as a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) solved via primal--dual Lagrangian relaxation with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), manifest lookahead, and buffer-stabilization cues.
On top of the learned policy we introduce a \emph{VMAF-aware runtime shield}: a deterministic projection layer that, upon emergency step-down, searches the bitrate ladder under buffer-feasibility constraints to maximize retained VMAF subject to a tunable perceptual-loss budget~$\tau$.

Paired trace-driven evaluation ($n{=}\VNepisodes$ episodes, identical policy and seeds) on an FCC/Norway-style broadband suite shows that the VMAF-aware shield simultaneously improves mean QoE, rebuffering, and VMAF over legacy index-decrement projection, with all three gains statistically significant at $\tau{=}0.8$ (paired Wilcoxon, $p{<}0.05$).
A sweep of $\VShieldVariants$ shield configurations collapses to $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes, identifying the VMAF-aware fallback as the dominant Pareto driver.
Out-of-distribution replay on synthetic 5G/mmWave stress traces corroborates significant QoE and rebuffer gains.
Removing the shield at inference inflates rebuffering substantially ($p{<}10^{-10}$), confirming policy--shield co-design as an operational necessity.
\end{abstract}

