% =========================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Client-side \emph{adaptive bitrate} (ABR) policies trained with deep reinforcement learning (DRL) can track high average surrogate rewards, yet they frequently learn aggressive bitrate choices that concentrate catastrophic rebuffer risk in the tail~\cite{mao2017neural,sutton2018reinforcement}.
Sub-6\,GHz and especially 5G mmWave access routinely inject millisecond-to-second capacity cliffs from blockage, LOS/NLOS transitions, and mobility~\cite{meng2024mmwave,cisco2023}, converting rare throughput dips into prolonged stalls that dominate viewer Quality of Experience (QoE) even when session-level averages appear acceptable~\cite{wu2024qoe,yan2019learning}.

Production stacks therefore layer \emph{runtime shields}---deterministic action projections executed before each chunk download---on top of learned policies~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.
The dominant legacy design is \emph{content-blind}: when buffer-depletion risk triggers, the shield applies uniform representation-index decrements until a viability test passes~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,mao2017neural}.
Because perceptual fidelity is not affine in the discrete bitrate index, such decrements routinely over-pay in VMAF for modest safety gains---adjacent ladder rungs can differ by a few VMAF points or by ten, depending on content and encoding~\cite{li2016vmaf}.
Operators are thus forced into an opaque safety--quality trade-off, even when a feasible downgrade path with negligible perceptual loss exists on the same manifest~\cite{xiao2025qoe,tian2022towards}.

In this paper we reposition the shield as an \emph{optimization layer} rather than a panic brake.
We introduce a \emph{VMAF-aware runtime shield}: a deterministic projection that, whenever an emergency step-down is required, searches the manifest-known bitrate ladder under buffer-margin feasibility constraints to maximize retained VMAF subject to a perceptual-loss budget~$\tau$ and a tunable catastrophic-ratio knob~$\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ (Sec.~\ref{sec:method:shield}).
The projection is a transparent, auditable function of chunk sizes, conservative throughput surrogates, buffer state, and per-chunk VMAF tables---not another opaque neural module~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,li2016vmaf}.

Paired trace-driven evaluation ($n{=}\VNepisodes$ episodes on identical policy and seeds) demonstrates that the VMAF-aware shield \emph{Pareto-improves} legacy index-decrement projection: at $\tau{=}0.8$, all three metrics---QoE, rebuffering, and VMAF---improve with statistical significance (paired Wilcoxon, $p{<}0.05$; Sec.~\ref{sec:eval}).
Out-of-distribution replay on synthetic 5G/mmWave stress traces corroborates significant QoE and rebuffer gains (Sec.~\ref{sec:eval:ood}), and removing the shield at inference from a shield-co-trained policy inflates rebuffering substantially, confirming policy--shield co-design as an operational necessity~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.

This paper makes the following contributions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{CMDP formulation with Lagrangian PPO.} We cast ABR as a Constrained Markov Decision Process with primal--dual updates over stall and switching surrogates, PPO, manifest lookahead, and buffer-regularization cues~\cite{altman1999constrained,paternain2019dual,schulman2017proximal}.

  \item \textbf{VMAF-aware runtime shielding.} We design a deterministic projection layer that optimizes emergency downgrade paths along nonlinear perceptual ladders, with auditable predicates and two operator-facing knobs ($\tau$, $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$; Sec.~\ref{sec:method:shield}).

  \item \textbf{Paired statistical validation.} Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on $n{=}\VNepisodes$ paired episodes establish a strictly significant QoE--rebuffer--VMAF triple at $\tau{=}0.8$, and 5G/mmWave stress replay confirms significant QoE and rebuffer improvements (Secs.~\ref{sec:eval}--\ref{sec:eval:ood}).

  \item \textbf{Policy--shield co-design evidence.} We show that constrained policies learn proposals whose safety is conditional on the shield at inference, and that a sweep of $\VShieldVariants$ configurations collapses to $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes dominated by the VMAF-aware fallback.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work; Section~\ref{sec:system} formalizes the system model and CMDP; Section~\ref{sec:method} details Lagrangian PPO and VMAF-aware projection; Section~\ref{sec:eval} presents the experimental evaluation; and Sections~\ref{sec:limits}--\ref{sec:conclusion} discuss limitations and conclude.

% =========================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

We structure the literature into three streams that culminate in the research gap our VMAF-aware shield targets.
Our contribution centers on \emph{client-side bitrate selection} under manifest and buffer observables---the control policy and its deterministic runtime projection---not on throughput estimation: data-driven predictors such as CS2P~\cite{sun2016cs2p} remain complementary infrastructure that any controller (including ours) may consume~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural}.
Recent surveys~\cite{bentaleb2018survey,wu2024qoe} and 5G/mmWave streaming measurement studies~\cite{meng2024mmwave} motivate why volatility-aware \emph{control} matters independently of marginal gains in mean throughput forecasts.

\subsection{Heuristic and model-based ABR (interpretable, tuning-limited)}

MPEG-DASH decouples packaging from transport; clients run local policies from ladder metadata and download observations~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}.
Buffer-threshold heuristics such as BBA~\cite{huang2014buffer} achieve remarkable simplicity and transparency, while BOLA-style online formulations expose Lyapunov-structured stability arguments for buffer evolution~\cite{spiteri2018bola}.
RobustMPC~\cite{yin2015control} epitomizes predictor-assisted constrained lookahead: bitrate is chosen by solving a short-horizon program under an estimated throughput trace.
\textbf{Strengths.} These designs are comparatively auditable: operators can reason about thresholds, horizons, and constraint violations without opening a neural graph.
\textbf{Limits toward 5G-class volatility.} Hand tuning does not scale across CDN regions, device classes, and blockage-heavy mmWave dynamics~\cite{meng2024mmwave,cisco2023}; when predictors lag abrupt capacity cliffs, even principled MPC objectives can mis-fire, and purely heuristic guards rarely exploit nonlinear perceptual ladders~\cite{li2016vmaf}.
Recent SIGCOMM work on smoothness-aware online control (SODA~\cite{chen2024soda}) sharpens production-relevant consistency objectives yet remains outside the DRL+shield co-design space we target.
Orthogonal systems layers---edge assistance~\cite{mehrabi2019edge,kim2023http,kang2023adaptive} and Oboe-style ABR auto-tuning~\cite{akhtar2018oboe}---change \emph{where} or \emph{how} knobs are calibrated, but do not by themselves resolve greedy neural tails or content-blind emergency projection.

\subsection{Deep reinforcement learning for ABR (expressive, opaque, tail-risk prone)}

Pensieve~\cite{mao2017neural} catalyzed trace-driven deep RL for ABR; contemporaneous and follow-on DRL formulations (e.g., D-DASH~\cite{gadaleta2017ddash}) demonstrate that neural policies can absorb heterogeneous histories and manifest features~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement}.
Recent PPO-flavored streaming RL~\cite{naresh2023ppoabr} and hybrid predictor--learner pipelines~\cite{liu2023hybrid} continue this trend into the 2023--2024 window emphasized by networking venues~\cite{wu2024qoe}.
\textbf{Strengths.} Scalarized RL objectives can track high average QoE surrogates on curated traces.
\textbf{Critical tension.} The policy network is a \emph{black box} to operators: maximizing expected return encourages aggressive bitrate choices whose failures concentrate in rare but catastrophic rebuffer tails under bursty cellular links~\cite{yan2019learning,mao2017neural}.
Field deployments (Fugu / learning in situ~\cite{yan2019learning}) further highlight distribution shift between simulators and live access networks.
These limitations motivate CMDP and safe RL formulations where declarative stall and smoothness budgets replace implicit reward scaling~\cite{xiao2025qoe,tian2022towards}.

\subsection{Safe / constrained RL, shields, and the content-blindness gap}

CMDPs and Lagrangian / primal--dual RL~\cite{altman1999constrained,paternain2019dual,achiam2017constrained} supply the optimization vocabulary for constraint-aware training; tutorial taxonomies categorize shields, Lyapunov methods, and risk-aware policy search~\cite{tian2022towards,chow2018lyapunov}.
QoE-oriented safe RL studies increasingly import these ideas into streaming~\cite{xiao2025qoe}.
Runtime shielding~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding} repairs or projects actions before environmental execution, yielding measurable intervention telemetry.
\emph{The unresolved gap.} In practice, streaming shields overwhelmingly implement content-blind representation-index decrements: when danger is detected, the client steps down the discrete ladder without considering how nonlinear VMAF spacing couples that decrement to perceptual damage~\cite{li2016vmaf}.
Such shields trade opaque amounts of visual fidelity for safety, even when an alternative feasible downgrade path could preserve nearly all VMAF~\cite{xiao2025qoe,tian2022towards}.
\emph{Our departure.} We treat the fallback itself as a perceptual optimization primitive: a deterministic VMAF-aware projection layer atop Lagrangian PPO, rather than another implicit QoE--stall compromise encoded in reward weights alone.

\textbf{Summary.}
Unlike prior work that treats safety projection as a content-blind mechanism, we design the shield to \emph{actively optimize} the emergency downgrade trajectory subject to buffer-feasibility tests, using manifest-known VMAF ladders~\cite{li2016vmaf,alshiekh2018shielding}.
To our knowledge, this is the first trace-driven ABR architecture to embed nonlinear perceptual metrics inside a deterministic runtime shield and to demonstrate a strict empirical Pareto improvement (paired QoE, rebuffer, VMAF) over legacy projection on identical policies and seeds (Sec.~\ref{sec:eval}).

