\section{System Model and Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:system}

\subsection{Streaming architecture and control loop}

We adopt the standard HTTP adaptive-streaming abstraction~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural,sodagar2011mpeg}: a \emph{video origin/CDN} exposes a discrete bitrate ladder \(\mathcal{B}=\{0,\ldots,L{-}1\}\) with per-chunk sizes and perceptual metadata in the DASH/HLS manifest; the \emph{client} maintains a playout buffer \(B_k\) and, at each chunk boundary \(k\), requests the next segment over a time-varying bottleneck whose effective throughput \(\hat{C}_k\) is exogenous to the controller in trace-driven emulation.
On sub-6\,GHz and especially mmWave access, \(\hat{C}_k\) can jump within milliseconds under blockage, LOS/NLOS transitions, and scheduling~\cite{meng2024mmwave,cisco2023}; this is the regime where greedy learned policies concentrate probability on tail stalls despite benign average traces~\cite{yan2019learning,mao2017neural}.

A neural policy \(\pi_\theta\) maps an augmented state \(s_k\) to a stochastic \emph{proposed} discrete action \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\in\mathcal{B}\) (sampled or argmax during evaluation).
Before the download of chunk \(k{+}1\) begins, the \emph{VMAF-aware runtime shield} \(\mathcal{S}\) applies a deterministic map
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:shield-map}
    \big(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k,\iota_k\big)=\mathcal{S}\!\left(s_k,a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\right),\qquad a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\in\mathcal{B},\ \ \iota_k\in\{0,1\},
\end{equation}
yielding the \emph{executed} representation index \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) and an audit bit \(\iota_k\) indicating whether projection altered the proposal.
Only \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) is passed to the chunk download and buffer dynamics~\eqref{eq:buffer}; Figure~\ref{fig:arch} highlights this \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\!\rightarrow\!\mathcal{S}\!\rightarrow\!a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) path.
Decisions evolve on the chunk clock (playback duration \(T_{\mathrm{c}}\) per step, e.g.\ \(4\,\mathrm{s}\) in our harness)~\cite{mao2017neural,yin2015control}.

Lagrangian PPO encodes stall/smoothness budgets as \emph{soft} expectations learned over many rollouts: dual variables \(\lambda_r,\lambda_s\) reweight penalties, but a neural policy alone offers no per-step certificate that a dangerously large representation will never be attempted---especially under out-of-training throughput swings~\cite{paternain2019dual,achiam2017constrained,meng2024mmwave}.
The shield supplies a \emph{hard}, deterministic repair map at every step: whatever \(\pi_\theta\) proposes, the client executes a manifest-feasible \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) that respects explicit buffer-margin viability tests~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.

\subsection{State, action, reward, and nonlinear perceptual mapping}

\emph{State \(s_k\).}
Beyond classical telemetry---buffer occupancy, past throughput samples, and the previous decision---the client observation includes \emph{manifest lookahead}: near-future chunk sizes across the ladder and spatial/temporal complexity tags (SI/TI) aligned with MPC-style horizon inputs~\cite{yin2015control,sodagar2011mpeg,mao2017neural}.
Crucially for VMAF-aware shielding, the state also exposes the \emph{per-chunk VMAF ladder} \(\{V_k(j)\}_{j\in\mathcal{B}}\): for chunk \(k\), \(V_k(j)\) is the perceptual score if representation \(j\) is fetched~\cite{li2016vmaf}.
If either the policy or the shield ignored \(\{V_k(j)\}\), content-dependent ladder spacing could not inform emergency downgrade, contradicting the premise of this paper.

\emph{Nonlinear, content-dependent fidelity.}
For each chunk \(k\), the map \(j\mapsto V_k(j)\) is not affine in the discrete index \(j\), nor is it time-invariant across \(k\): different GOP/texture structure yields different marginal VMAF gains between adjacent rungs.
The composition ``nominal bitrate \(\rightarrow\) VMAF'' is therefore a nonlinear, piecewise, content-varying mapping, which is why legacy index-decrement shields can inflict disproportionate perceptual loss relative to a VMAF-aware search over the same feasible set~\cite{li2016vmaf}.

\emph{Action.}
\(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\in\mathcal{B}\) is the policy's proposed bitrate index for chunk \(k{+}1\); the environment observes only \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\).

\emph{Benchmarking reward (QoE surrogate).}
Following RobustMPC/Pensieve reporting conventions~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural}, we log a scalar per-chunk surrogate with fidelity \(q_k \equiv V_k\!\left(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\right)\):
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:qoe-rep}
    \mathrm{QoE}^{\mathrm{(rep)}}_{k} = q_k - \alpha\, \mathrm{smooth}_k - \beta\, \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k,
\end{equation}
where \(\mathrm{smooth}_k=\lvert q_k-q_{k-1}\rvert\) proxies quality switching, \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\) is rebuffer seconds on step \(k\), and \(\alpha,\beta\) match the shared evaluation harness.
Session QoE is \(\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_e}\mathrm{QoE}^{\mathrm{(rep)}}_{k}\) as in Section~\ref{sec:eval}.
The \emph{CMDP objective} below separates primal fidelity \(\sum q_k\) from explicit constraint channels on stall/smooth statistics~\cite{altman1999constrained,paternain2019dual}.

\subsection{Chunk-timescale dynamics}

\subsubsection{Throughput and latency}
Let \(\hat{C}_k>0\) denote the effective throughput for chunk \(k{+}1\) (trace-driven, comparable to baselines).
Choosing \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) induces payload size \(\sigma_{k}(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k)\) from the manifest; with \(\tilde{C}_k=\max\{\hat{C}_k,\epsilon\}\), \(\epsilon{>}0\), nominal download seconds are \(d_k=\sigma_{k}(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k)/\tilde{C}_k\)~\cite{yin2015control}.

\subsubsection{Buffer and stall}
Let \(B_k\ge 0\) be buffer seconds at the start of download \(k\)~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural}.
Rebuffering (stall) and buffer update follow
\begin{equation}
    \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k=\max\{0,\, d_k-B_k\},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:buffer}
    B_{k+1}=\max\{0,B_k-d_k\}+T_{\mathrm{c}}.
\end{equation}
Stochasticity enters through \(\hat{C}_k\) on curated traces~\cite{fcc2015mba,kvalbein2014,bentaleb2018survey}; given \((s_k,a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k,\hat{C}_k)\), transitions are deterministic.

\subsection{CMDP costs, safety budgets, and Lagrangian relaxation}

An unconstrained MDP maximizes \(\mathbb{E}[\sum_k \gamma^k r_k]\) alone.
We treat streaming as a \emph{Constrained MDP} (CMDP): maximize expected fidelity subject to budgets on costs induced by the same trajectory~\cite{altman1999constrained}.
Define instantaneous stall cost \(c^{\mathrm{stall}}_k \equiv \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\) (seconds of stall on step \(k\)) and smoothness cost \(c^{\mathrm{sm}}_k \equiv \mathrm{smooth}_k\).
A canonical discounted formulation requires, for discount \(\gamma\in(0,1]\) and budgets \(d_{\mathrm{rf}},d_{\mathrm{sm}}\),
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cmdp-disc}
    \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\rho}\Big[\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_e} \gamma^{k-1} c^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\Big] \le d_{\mathrm{rf}},
    \qquad
    \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\rho}\Big[\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_e} \gamma^{k-1} c^{\mathrm{sm}}_k\Big] \le d_{\mathrm{sm}},
\end{equation}
Our implementation follows undiscounted episodic summaries for logging and dual updates: normalized stall ratio \(\mathrm{RB}(e)\) and average \(\mathrm{SM}(e)\), which are positive linear functionals of \(\{c^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\}\) and \(\{c^{\mathrm{sm}}_k\}\) over \(\mathcal{K}_e=\{1,\ldots,K_e\}\)~\cite{mao2017neural,paternain2019dual}.
Let \(T^{\mathrm{play}}_{e}=K_{e}T_{\mathrm{c}}\). Then
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{RB}(e)=\frac{1}{T^{\mathrm{play}}_{e}}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} c^{\mathrm{stall}}_k,
    \qquad
    \mathrm{SM}(e)=\frac{1}{K_{e}}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} c^{\mathrm{sm}}_k.
\end{equation}
The constrained program motivating training is
\begin{align}
\label{eq:prim}
    \max_{\pi_\theta} ~ & \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\,\rho}\Big[\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} q_k\Big],\\
\label{eq:c1}
    \mathrm{s.t.} ~~ & \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\,\rho}\Big[\mathrm{RB}(e)\Big] \le \delta_{\mathrm{rf}},\\
\label{eq:c2}
    & \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\,\rho}\Big[\mathrm{SM}(e)\Big] \le \delta_{\mathrm{sm}},
\end{align}
with targets \(\delta_{\mathrm{rf}},\delta_{\mathrm{sm}}\) fixed for reproducibility~\cite{yin2015control}.

Because directly enforcing~\eqref{eq:c1}--\eqref{eq:c2} inside non-convex policy search is intractable, we form the Lagrangian relaxation with per-step penalty channels \(\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k,\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\):
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:L}
    \mathcal{L}(\theta,\lambda) =
    \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\,\rho}\Big[\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} \big( q_k - \lambda_r\, \widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k - \lambda_s\, \widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k \big)\Big],
\end{equation}
with \(\lambda_r,\lambda_s\ge 0\) adapted by projected dual ascent on empirical gaps \(g_r=\hat{\mathrm{RB}}-\delta_{\mathrm{rf}}\), \(g_s=\hat{\mathrm{SM}}-\delta_{\mathrm{sm}}\)~\cite{paternain2019dual,altman1999constrained}.
Equivalently, the \emph{instantaneous} reward used inside PPO can be read as \(q_k - \lambda_r \widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k - \lambda_s \widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\) (plus optional stabilizers; Sec.~\ref{sec:method})---a standard ``reward minus \(\lambda\cdot\)cost'' construction.
Finite-sample PPO with clipped objectives does not certify constraint satisfaction at every iterate~\cite{schulman2017proximal,achiam2017constrained}; that gap motivates the hard shield in~\eqref{eq:shield-map}.

\emph{Remark (auxiliary Lyapunov cues).}
Training optionally subtracts buffer-deviation and simulator Lyapunov penalties~\cite{neely2010stochastic} outside \((\lambda_r,\lambda_s)\); these are stabilizers, not formal certificates.

\subsection{VMAF-aware shield as a hard projection}

Let \(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe},k}(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)\subseteq\mathcal{B}\) denote the set of representation indices that pass the shield's buffer-margin and catastrophic-download viability tests at step \(k\) (Sec.~\ref{sec:method:shield}), instantiated with pessimistic throughput, manifest sizes \(\sigma_k(\cdot)\), and buffer \(B_k\).
\emph{Legacy content-blind repair} maps \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\) downward by repeated index decrements until a feasible index is found~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.

\emph{VMAF-aware projection.}
Among feasible rungs, we retain the maximal perceptual score subject to a VMAF-loss budget \(\tau\) relative to the proposal:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:shield-argmax}
    a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k \in
    \arg\max_{\;j\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe},k}(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)\,:\; V_k(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)-V_k(j)\le \tau\;}
    V_k(j),
\end{equation}
with a strict light/strong fallback if~\eqref{eq:shield-argmax} is empty~\cite{li2016vmaf}.
Thus \(\mathcal{S}\) is a deterministic perceptual projection \(\pi_{\mathrm{shield}}:(s_k,a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)\mapsto a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) that optimizes retained VMAF on \(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe},k}\) instead of blindly stepping down the index order.
Full predicate details (catastrophic ratio \(\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\), pessimism \(\rho\), margin \(\mu\)) appear in Section~\ref{sec:method:shield}.

% =========================================
\section{Method: Lagrangian PPO and Runtime Shields}
\label{sec:method}

This section provides the implementation details needed to reproduce training and shielding.
We build on Stable-Baselines~3 PPO~\cite{raffin2021sb3,schulman2017proximal} with a custom Lagrangian reward wrapper and two deterministic shield variants: a QoE-oriented core (Algorithm~\ref{alg:shield-v13}) and a VMAF-aware perceptual projection (Algorithm~\ref{alg:shield-vmaf}).
Symbols follow Section~\ref{sec:system}: horizon \(k=1,\ldots,K_e\), \(T_{\mathrm{c}}\), \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k,a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\in\mathcal{B}\), \(B_k\), \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\), buffering~\eqref{eq:buffer}, and logged penalties \(\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k,\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\).

\subsection{Observation interface and policy outputs}

State \(s_k\) and the shield map \(\mathcal{S}\) are defined in Section~\ref{sec:system}; the actor--critic stack consumes the same tensors as Figure~\ref{fig:arch} (throughput history, buffer/last action, manifest lookahead including SI/TI and per-chunk VMAF ladders)~\cite{mao2017neural,sodagar2011mpeg,yin2015control,li2016vmaf}.
The actor outputs a categorical distribution over raw proposals \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\in\mathcal{B}\); optional hysteresis or shield-aware shaping wrappers may alter sampling incentives, but the executed action is always \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k=\mathcal{S}(s_k,a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)\) when shielding is enabled~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.

\subsection{Lagrangian PPO agent with Lyapunov-style buffer regularization}\label{sec:method:ppo}

\textbf{PPO backbone.}
We use standard clipped PPO~\cite{schulman2017proximal} with twin Tanh MLP heads for \(\pi_\theta(a\mid s_k)\) and \(V_\phi(s_k)\), GAE \((\gamma,\lambda_{\mathrm{GAE}})\), surrogate clipping, entropy bonuses, and Adam schedules (Table~\ref{tab:hypers})~\cite{raffin2021sb3}.

\textbf{CMDP shaping and stability cues.}
The train-time signal is the Lagrangian-relaxed objective~\eqref{eq:L} instantiated per step by~\eqref{eq:rtrain}.
In addition to the dual-weighted stall/smooth channels, we inject Lyapunov-style buffer regularizers \(\phi(B_k)\) and emulator drift cues \(\psi^{\mathrm{Lya}}_k\) with fixed coefficients \((w_b,w_\ell)\) outside the dual pair \((\lambda_r,\lambda_s)\)~\cite{neely2010stochastic,chow2018lyapunov}.
The regularizer \(\phi\) penalizes dangerous buffer deviations early in training, tempering exploratory bitrate spikes before dual variables fully adapt; \(\psi^{\mathrm{Lya}}\) surfaces simulator-provided drift cues analogous to queue-stability heuristics.
These terms are not formal safety certificates; rather, they reduce variance and premature dual divergence during trace-driven CMDP training~\cite{paternain2019dual,tian2022towards}.

