Removing the Lagrangian constraint mechanism (``$-$ Lagrangian'') more than doubles mean rebuffering from $14.73\%$ to $31.46\%$ while marginally raising VMAF, confirming that unconstrained optimization concentrates gains in fidelity at the expense of catastrophic tail stalls.
Removing manifest lookahead (``$-$ Lookahead'') reduces QoE by $45$ points and doubles the switching rate, indicating that future chunk-size information helps the policy plan smoother bitrate trajectories.
Removing Lyapunov regularizers (``$-$ Lyapunov'') slightly lowers rebuffering ($11.63\%$ vs.\ $14.73\%$) but drops VMAF by $1.4$ points, suggesting that the buffer-deviation penalty steers the policy toward higher-fidelity representations at a modest stall cost.
No single ablated variant dominates the full policy: the reported operating points emerge from the combined CMDP objective, not from any single auxiliary feature.

\textbf{Operational overhead.} The shield performs deterministic arithmetic over the bitrate ladder, manifest chunk sizes, buffer state, conservative throughput surrogates, and (when VMAF-aware) a small per-rung VMAF lookup. Its computational cost is negligible relative to chunk download times and network RTTs.

Figure~\ref{fig:ablation:scatter} visualizes the QoE--rebuffer trade-off for each ablated variant, confirming that the full CMDP policy occupies a distinct region of the Pareto front.

