\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

\textbf{Scope.}
This paper contributes a deployable, auditable QoE-vs-stall trade space: runtime shields parameterized by a VMAF-loss budget~$\tau$ and a catastrophic-ratio knob~$\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ that operators can tune post-deployment without retraining.
We do not prove formal worst-case safety guarantees, nor do we claim QoE dominance over every ABR baseline at every operating point.
The statistical evidence (Section~\ref{sec:eval}) establishes that the VMAF-aware family Pareto-improves legacy projection, substantially reduces rebuffering relative to Pensieve and RobustMPC, and that the design space collapses to a low-dimensional family.

\textbf{Threats to validity.}
\emph{(i)}~The evaluation is simulation-based and may not capture live cellular dynamics, TCP/QUIC stack effects, or CDN cache behavior~\cite{meng2024mmwave}.
\emph{(ii)}~The test set covers four videos and a finite set of traces; larger-scale trace diversity is needed before deployment claims~\cite{kvalbein2014}.
\emph{(iii)}~QoE is measured via a VMAF-based surrogate; subjective user studies may rank stall, fidelity, and switching differently~\cite{wu2024qoe}.
\emph{(iv)}~The behavioral-class collapse ($\VShieldBehaviors$ classes) is conditioned on this policy, ladder, and trace mix; alternative ladders (e.g., HDR) may warrant re-characterization.
\emph{(v)}~The policy--shield co-design dependence means recommended operating points require the shield at inference (Table~\ref{tab:pareto:family}).
\emph{(vi)}~The synthetic 5G/mmWave suite isolates throughput volatility but lacks stack realism; under stall-saturated conditions, VMAF ceases to be a sharp paired discriminator~\cite{lakens2018equivalence}.
\emph{(vii)}~Genie uses non-causal trace knowledge and serves only as an oracle reference.

\textbf{Future work.}
Promising directions include live or packet-level emulation (e.g., Mahimahi), broader mobile/fixed-broadband trace families, per-trace tail metrics, risk-sensitive objectives such as CVaR~\cite{rockafellar2000}, uncertainty-aware throughput prediction, and \emph{adaptive} $\tau$-scheduling from live telemetry---moving along the Pareto front in response to observed QoE budgets.

% =========================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented a VMAF-aware runtime shield for constrained deep RL in adaptive video streaming.
The shield is a deterministic action-projection layer that, upon emergency step-down, selects the highest-VMAF feasible rung subject to a perceptual-loss budget~$\tau$ rather than uniformly decrementing the bitrate index.
Combined with a Lagrangian CMDP training pipeline, the system provides two interpretable deployment knobs ($\tau$ and $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$) that operators can tune without retraining.

Paired trace-driven evaluation ($n{=}\VNepisodes$) on an FCC/Norway-style broadband suite demonstrates that the VMAF-aware shield achieves a strict empirical three-way Pareto improvement over legacy index-decrement projection: at $\tau{=}0.8$, QoE, rebuffering, and VMAF all improve with $p{<}0.05$ (Wilcoxon signed-rank).
Out-of-distribution replay on synthetic 5G/mmWave stress traces corroborates significant QoE and rebuffer gains.
A sweep of $\VShieldVariants$ shield configurations collapses to $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes, identifying the VMAF-aware fallback---the projection \emph{shape}---as the dominant Pareto driver.
Removing the shield at inference inflates rebuffering substantially ($p{<}10^{-10}$), confirming that constrained policies and their shields form a single deployable unit.

Future work will target live or packet-level validation, risk-sensitive objectives (CVaR~\cite{rockafellar2000}), uncertainty-aware throughput prediction, and adaptive scheduling of the VMAF-loss tolerance from deployment telemetry.

